  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nToday - Monday: Frost Tonight & Monday Morning in North-Central WI\n\nUpper-level northwesterly flow with surface high pressure building \ninto the region will be the main show for Sunday allowing for \nsky cover to steadily decrease throughout much of the daytime \nhours. As we head later into the evening and overnight, surface \nhigh pressure moves overhead which will allow winds to trend \nlight and variable overnight. Consequently, the combination of \nlight winds, clear skies, and 850mb temperatures falling to \naround 2-4 C will allow for some patchy to areas of frost across\nthe local area. Overall, the NBM has very high probabilities \n(60-80%) for low temperatures to fall below 37 degrees across \nmuch of north-central and west-central WI with some locations \nin typical cold drainage areas potentially approaching the \nfreezing mark. Therefore, have hoisted a Frost Advisory in \ntypical cold spots of Taylor, Clark, Jackson, Monroe, Juneau, \nand Adams Counties. \n\nTuesday - Friday: Dry with Near to Slightly Above Normal \nTemperatures\n\nOtherwise, as we head later into the week, temperatures will remain \nrelatively steady-state with persistent northwesterly flow across \nthe region and a relatively unchanged synoptic pattern. \nConsequently, median temperatures in the NBM remain relatively \nconsistent with highs in the 60s to lower 70s through Friday. \nAdditionally, with the lack of any substantial synoptic forcing in \nthe northwesterly flow regime, thinking that precipitation chances \nwill be fairly minimal. This is supported by the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) which has minimal probabilities (under \n20% chance) for measurable rainfall through Friday across the \nregion. It is possible that if a shortwave descending into the Great \nLakes on Tuesday manages to trek slightly further south, \nprecipitation probabilities may increase slightly. However, ensemble \nguidance generally keeps the highest measurable precipitation \nchanges well north of the local area.",
